From: Jacob Cook <jacobmcook1984@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: sun4i-usb: disable the clock that was enabled for PHY2

sun4i_usb_phy_init() enables phy2->clk2 to reach PHY2's REG_HCI_PHY_CTL,
then disables phy->clk2 instead of phy2->clk2. The clock just enabled
keeps a reference for the life of the PHY, and the one belonging to the
PHY being initialised is disabled while still in use. On H700 that is
the USB PHY left running across suspend. Xu Rao posted the same fix
to linux-arm-kernel in July 2026; take it until it lands.

Link: https://lists.infradead.org/pipermail/linux-arm-kernel/2026-July/1145685.html
Reported-by: kailashrs <6493816+kailashrs@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git c/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c i/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c
index 201d172082a7..5e826a3c0244 100644
--- c/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c
+++ i/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@ static int sun4i_usb_phy_init(struct phy *_phy)
 			writel(val, phy2->pmu + REG_HCI_PHY_CTL);
 		}
 
-		clk_disable_unprepare(phy->clk2);
+		clk_disable_unprepare(phy2->clk2);
 	}
 
 	if (phy->pmu && data->cfg->hci_phy_ctl_clear) {
